Make the PPE actually bind flows, and tell it what it is looking at.

Everything below was found by measuring against the engine on hardware;
the reasoning for each piece is in the comment beside it.

 - The FOE entry stride is 64 bytes here, not the 80 upstream uses, and
   the table is little-endian while this CPU is not, so entries are
   byte-swapped on the way in and out. A whole-word reversal exchanges
   the halves of every 32-bit word, so each u16 pair has to be declared
   back-to-front relative to upstream.
 - act_dp, the egress port, is a single byte at offset 40 for the IPv4
   class -- the old Ralink layout, where upstream has udf_tsid.
 - nf_flow_table_offload hands the two halves of a MAC address as
   arithmetic, not bytes, so reaching for them with a fixed "+ 2" picks
   the empty half on a big-endian host. Every offloaded flow went out
   addressed to xx:xx:xx:xx:00:00.
 - The DSA tag is reproduced by the PPE's VLAN layer rather than by the
   tagger, and the GDM needs the VLAN ethertype and its special tag
   enabled before it will hand a tagged frame to the engine at all.
 - The RX descriptor's ppe_entry does not address the slot the engine
   wrote. en75_ppe_hash_entry() reproduces the engine's own index
   instead, so a flow is bound where it will be found, the moment the
   flowtable offers it.

Forwarding goes from 275 Mbit/s at 64% CPU idle to 937 Mbit/s at 99.8%.

